module bindings.rust.condvar_notify_without_guard;

// A condition variable pairs with the mutex that guards its predicate. The
// waiter must check the predicate and block while holding the guard, because
// wait releases the mutex and sleeps atomically; the matching obligation on
// the signalling side is to keep that guard live across the predicate write
// and the notify. Rust's temporary rules make the violating shape easy to
// write:
//
//     *self.mutex.lock().unwrap() = true;   // guard drops at the semicolon
//     self.cond.notify_one();               // notify with the mutex unlocked
//
// Storing through the lock() temporary ends the guard's lifetime at the end of
// the statement, so a waiter that checks the flag between the store and the
// notify then blocks on a notification that has already fired and sleeps
// forever -- the lost wakeup. Binding the guard to a variable
// (`let mut flag = self.mutex.lock().unwrap(); *flag = true;
// self.cond.notify_one();`) keeps the mutex held across the notify and is the
// form this binding stays quiet on.
//
// The store is recognised through the assignment token, whose left side is the
// lock() call itself; a method call through the temporary
// (`m.lock().unwrap().push(x)`) emits no assignment token and is not seen.
// The `.await` exclusion is not cosmetic: an async lock is not a
// std::sync::Mutex guard, and the notifiers it pairs with (tokio::sync::Notify
// and peers) are permit-based with no check-then-block window, so the
// lost-wakeup protocol does not apply to them.
binding condvarNotifyWithoutGuard {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "rust" and node.context.call in ["notify_one", "notify_all"] and node.context.assign contains "lock()" and not (node.context.assign contains "lock().await")
  emit issue code.CondvarNotifyWithoutGuard at node
}
